<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prayagraj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Allahabad, Prayag</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prayagraj, called Allahabad from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> until 2018, is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>, a sacred bathing place for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>. It hosts the Kumbh Mela, one of the largest religious gatherings in the world. Its fort holds the Ashokan pillar bearing the Allahabad inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a>, and it was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prayagraj.jpg" alt="Prayagraj" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dananuj · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allahabad_Montage_II_Dec_2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 1 November 1858 Lord Canning, the first viceroy, read Queen Victoria’s proclamation at <b>Allahabad</b>, promising religious toleration, equal access to office and respect for the rights of the Indian princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors Trilochanapala and Yashahpala ruled a small territory around Kannauj and <b>Allahabad</b>, and Yashahpala’s inscription of 1036 is the last record of the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…authority contracted to the Punjab, Gandhara and Kashmir, while Mathura and the Ganges valley passed to local powers such as the Nagas and later to the Gupta Empire; the <b>Allahabad</b> pillar inscription of Samudragupta mentions the homage of a 'Daivaputra-Shahi-Shahanushahi', generally taken to be a late Kushan ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the mid-fourth century the <b>Allahabad</b> pillar inscription of Samudragupta lists Vishnugopa of Kanchi among the southern kings defeated during the Gupta ruler's southern campaign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…book claimed that it had been a Hindu temple, but India's Supreme Court dismissed his petition in 2000, the <b>Allahabad</b> High Court dismissed a similar one in 2005, and in 2017 the Archaeological Survey of India declared that there was no evidence it had ever housed a temple.</blockquote><a href="/work/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1930, in his presidential address to the Muslim League's annual conference at <b>Allahabad</b>, he supported a separate nation-state in the Muslim-majority areas of the subcontinent, an idea that inspired the creation of Pakistan.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha</a></li></ul></section>
